\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
Torona (ISO 639-3 tqr; Glottocode toro1251) is a Talodi language of South Kordofan, Sudan. Glottolog lists it as extinct, and Ethnologue labels it dormant. According to Wikipedia, the last speaker died in January 2014, and the community has shifted to Tira. Very little exists beyond a few academic descriptions. No school materials were found.

\section{Primary Educational Resources}
None found. No primers, textbooks or school programmes for Torona turned up in the searches.

\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item Torona language (Wikipedia overview, which cites the main description): \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Torona_language}
\item Norton and Alaki (2016), ``Torona: a disappearing Talodi language of Sudan'' (WOCAL-7 proceedings), listed in Glottolog as the main reference: \url{https://glottolog.org/resource/reference/id/546884}
\item Glottolog entry for Torona: \url{https://glottolog.org/resource/languoid/id/toro1251}
\item Norton and Alaki (2015) source record at ASJP: \url{https://asjp.clld.org/sources/64}
\item Parallel Grammar Documentation in Four Talodi Languages (a journal article on related languages): \url{https://journals.ub.uni-koeln.de/index.php/AAeo/en/article/view/3457}
\item Talodi language (related language, Wikipedia): \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Talodi_language}
\end{itemize}

\section{Religious and Community Resources}
None found.

\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
No audio or digital resources for Torona were found in the searches.

\section{Curriculum Development Context}
There are no living first-language speakers, so any curriculum would have to be a heritage or documentation project built on Norton and Alaki's description and any recordings or word lists they hold. Tira is the community's current language. This report found no existing educational materials.
\end{document}
